<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Avaris</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tell el-Dab'a</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Avaris was an ancient city in the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, at the modern site of Tell el-Dab'a. From the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a> a large population of Levantine origin settled there, and it became the seat of the Fourteenth Dynasty and then the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> kings of the Fifteenth Dynasty, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i">Ahmose I</a> captured it around 1550 BCE. Excavations since 1966 have found Minoan-style wall paintings there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/avaris.jpg" alt="Avaris" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Dahl; derivative work by MinisterForBadTimes · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lower_Egypt-en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Avaris, in Egyptian Hut-waret, probably meaning House of the Region, lay at the modern site of Tell el-Dab'a in the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>. As the main course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> migrated eastward, its position at the hub of the Delta's trading centres made it well suited as a capital. Its closeness to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> made it a popular town for Asiatic immigrants, who were mostly culturally Egyptianized but kept parts of their own culture, as burials with weapons of Levantine origin show.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The site was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-i">Amenemhat I</a>, around 1930 BCE according to one chronology, as a small Egyptian town. It began to grow around 1830 BCE with the immigration of Canaanites, and by 1800 BCE it was a much larger trade colony under Egyptian control. A temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seth">Seth</a> was built about 1780 BCE; the Canaanites there took Seth for their weather god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Hadad</a>. About 1700 BCE a temple district to the Canaanite Asherah and the Egyptian Hathor was built, and from then on social stratification and an elite appear.</p><p>In the 18th century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> and made Avaris their capital. The city grew to 250 hectares and is believed to have been the largest in the world from 1670 to 1557 BCE. Kamose, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the Seventeenth Dynasty, besieged it without success. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i">Ahmose I</a> captured it in the 16th century BCE, and Canaanite-style objects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> suggest that a large part of its Semitic population stayed. The palace district was briefly abandoned when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Pi-Ramesses</a> about 2 km to the north, Avaris became a cemetery and a major naval base, and its harbour kept its name through the Ramesside period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Édouard Naville began excavating near Tell el-Daba in 1885, and in 1941 and 1942 Labib Habachi proposed that the site was Avaris. The Austrian Archaeological Institute has excavated it since 1966. In 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radar">radar</a> imaging showed the outline of the city, with streets, houses, a port and a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> through it. Excavations show a trading centre with a harbour that served over 300 ships in a season.</p><p>A temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> period yielded goods from all over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> world and wall paintings in the style of the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. The Austrian archaeologist Manfred Bietak has suggested close contact between Avaris's rulers and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minoan-civilisation">Minoans</a>, and Yves Duhoux has proposed a Minoan colony on an island in the Delta.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Avaris” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Meanwhile people of Levantine origin had settled in the eastern Delta around <b>Avaris</b> (Tell el-Dab'a), and a separate line, the Fourteenth Dynasty of Egypt, ruled there in the eighteenth century BCE, from a date estimated between about 1805 and 1725 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Hyksos period, when chariot-mounted maryannu ruled Egypt from <b>Avaris</b>, Baal was identified with the Egyptian god Set and was thereafter shown wearing the crown of Lower Egypt, while Asherah, Astarte and Anat were given Hathor-like wigs.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Wall paintings in Minoan style have been found far from Crete, at Tel Kabri in present-day Israel and at <b>Tell el-Dab’a</b>, the ancient Avaris in the Nile Delta, where they include scenes of young athletes leaping over bulls.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Soldiers were paid, and the brave won the “gold of valour” and captives: Ahmose, son of Ebana, who fought the Hyksos at <b>Avaris</b>, received gold and slaves from his kings.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1650 BCE rulers of Levantine descent, called Hyksos from the Egyptian for 'rulers of foreign lands', took power at <b>Avaris</b> in the eastern Delta and captured Memphis, ending the Middle Kingdom of Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/fifteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Fifteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Thirteenth Dynasty lost its grip, around 1725 BCE a separate line of kings established itself in the eastern Delta, probably at <b>Avaris</b> (Tell el-Dab'a), where a large population of Levantine origin had settled.</blockquote><a href="/state/fourteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Fourteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
